WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.222
Suppose a sample of visits gives an
average lab waiting time of eighteen

00:00:04.222 --> 00:00:04.691
minutes.

00:00:04.911 --> 00:00:08.876
How much uncertainty is there in our
estimate of the population mean?

00:00:09.526 --> 00:00:13.476
In this example, the method gives a
margin of error of two minutes.

00:00:13.696 --> 00:00:18.103
Subtract two from eighteen to get
sixteen. Add two to get twenty. The

00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:20.976
interval runs from sixteen to twenty
minutes.

00:00:21.626 --> 00:00:26.270
Imagine repeating the same sampling and
interval method. Each new sample can

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.980
produce different endpoints.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.726
The vertical line marks the fictional
population mean, eighteen point five

00:00:32.726 --> 00:00:35.294
minutes. The first two intervals cross
it.

00:00:35.944 --> 00:00:40.363
The third interval ends at seventeen
point five. It misses the fixed mean of

00:00:40.363 --> 00:00:41.526
eighteen point five.

00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:46.105
These three intervals are a drawing. A
well-calibrated ninety-five percent

00:00:46.105 --> 00:00:50.347
method covers the true mean about
ninety-five percent of the time across

00:00:50.347 --> 00:00:52.703
many repetitions, under its assumptions.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:57.780
The ninety-five percent describes the
method's long-run coverage. Once you

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.489
have sampled, the endpoints and the
population mean are fixed.

00:01:01.709 --> 00:01:06.162
More independent observations can
improve precision. A narrow interval can

00:01:06.162 --> 00:01:09.050
still mislead if your sampling method is
biased.
